# ---- PAD-379: the shield platform (Premium/LE only; a Pro's shield targets are fixed) ---------------------------
# The three shield targets sit on a platform a motor turns: ShieldMotor (vtable 0x6502a0, a SingleDirectionCoilMotor:
# SHIELD MOTOR, node 9 coil 2, runs one way until a position switch), one object at 0x7bbf88. Two positions, from its
# table at 0x650330: switch 86 "Shield Motor Open" - the spinner side faces the player, the shields away (the game's
# home, where most battles play) - and switch 87 "Shield Motor Closed" - the shield targets face the flippers
# (parts of Mechagodzilla multiball; RuleMechagodzillaShield counts a shield hit only while +44 is 87). The move is the
# motor's v[29] (SingleDirectionCoilMotor's go-to, 0x1db3d8): (motor, position switch) refuses when the operator
# switched the motor off (v[15], SHIELD MOTOR DISABLED), returns 1 when it is already there, else starts the move
# process (0x1db46c): the target at +48, the coil run with that switch as its stop, +44 the switch on arrival. The
# service menu's shield test calls the same through v[9] (0x1db36c), which first insists the caller is the motor's
# owner process. +44 is the switch it last stopped on (0 = not known: its homing sends it to 86). NOT 0x1da004: that
# only stores +50, a position the move process assumes when a move fails (emulator run shield1: the motor never ran).
# pm_shield / pm_shield_position.
site shield_move           0x001db3d8 0xe92d4038 0xe1a05001
data shield_motor          0x007bbf88
value shield_motor_vptr    0x6502a8
value shield_pos_at        44
value shield_target_at     48
value shield_away          86
value shield_toward        87
